Subject: SOC/TECH: Viral Assault

Most employers punish employees for NOT going to work. For one thing,
some companies limit the number of sick days a year you can take, and
may fire an employee who takes more than six. For another thing, some
people are paid close enough to subsistence wages that they literally
cannot afford to take time off if they are not paid for sick days.
People with children have an even more difficult problem.

Perhaps there ought to be a mechanism for paying people to stay home?
Instead of putting in monitors, how about establishing a pool of funds
that an employee can draw on? Perhaps with some quick
proof-of-eligibility. 
  I don't usually agree with my union (starting with the question of
whether I should BE in a union...the job went union after I took it)
but one thing we have here is a "sick days" pool where employees can
contribute sick days and are then eligible to withdraw as many as they
need. I think this works out to taking your sick days as personal days
and taking your real sick time from the bank....(I'm not a bank
member, btw) Anyway I'm not sure that the sick days bank is a good
idea but it does seem to let people be sick. Of course, some
self-sacrificing souls will com ein out of a sense of "duty" and
infect us all anyway. 
   I throw sneezers out of my office and it always surprises them. 
Especially the students...but I don't value a five-minute conversation
with any of them enough to get sick for two weeks. Between the
students and the subway I get sick much more often as it is.




