Subject: ECON/PHIL:  _CORPORATE_ Socialism

_CORPORATE_ Socialism

Garrett Goebel has raised an important issue, the support for kinds of
socialism by large corporations. In his words:

>To continue, I whole heartedly agree with Krzys' that socialism is a 
>modern tool for enslavement, but I believe I differ with regards to who
>is being enslaved.  I believe that used properly, socialism is a tool
>that the capitalist uses to enslave those who would revolt, into the
>system of the market economy.

Large, entrenched corporations may see several advantages to having various
forms of increased government regulation and general socialism:

1. Increased regulation helps them against smaller, upstart competitors.
Intel Corporation has entire buildings full of people filling out forms on
health issues, unemployment insurance, miscellaneous compliance issues,
and, of course, many kinds of taxes. NewChip, Inc., a small startup, faces
tremendous obstacles in complying with the many laws and regulations.

2. Increased socialism allows large companies to generally consolidate
their status, for the reason just cited, and for the reason that the
government tends to prop up the existing companies ("industrial policy").
Lockheed and Chrysler are perfect examples.

(Look for talk of "saving IBM, a national asset.")

3. Large companies can exploit government largesse. The "Sematech"
boondoggle in Austin, Texas, is essentially of no use to smaller companies
like Cypress Semiconductor, a point argued persuasively by T.J. Rodgers,
their President. The rewards go to the large chip companies, though the
population as a whole, and the small companies, are taxed to pay for it.
This is just subsidized research for corporate socialists.

4. As part of the "deal" to semi-socialize a corporation (Chrysler, various
French companies, etc.), concessions are forced upon labor and other
participants. This controls the unions (we anarcho-capitalists may have a
built-in bias against unions, thinking of them as collectivist entities,
but having the government ban union organizing should be despised by all
freedom-loving folks.). 

Corporations often appreciate this kind of government intervention. As
usual, the largest companies are favored.

5. Freezing in place. This repeats parts of the points above, but is worth
stating separately. Corporate socialism essentially locks-in the market
status of companies. Managers, and even shareholders, may actually prefer
this "safe" route, so long as their pensions, stock prices, etc., are
upheld. (Large companies usually end up being run be those _hired_ by the
founders. They are essentially salaried bureaucrats. Having the government
step in and freeze things is perhaps the ideal outcome for them.)

(Outright nationalization, as in total seizure of assets, is of course not
desired by many (though most nationalized companies keep on the managers,
so perhaps even this is not really the case). But even most
"nationalizations," I suspect, are done on reasonably favorable terms and
may not be fought very hard by the owners. They may see a way to get the
government to acquire their struggling enterprise. (Bogosity warning: I
could be completely wrong on this "hunch," as I have no way to study the
numbers in enough cases. Some real economists out there may have some
numbers to support or refute this hunch.)

The recent talk of national health care in the U.S. is related to these
trends. Corporations are looking for various ways to have the government
pay their employee expenses (which are often mandated by law, so the
corporations _do_ have a reasonable gripe, but that's another debate).

It's not at all surprising that major companies supported Bill Clinton.

I look for corporations to be the major lobbying groups for new government
regulation in the future. As usual, they'll mouth platitudes about free
enterprise, mostly to buttress their bargaining position as they attempt to
maximize government largesse.

A Faustian bargain even for high-tech enthusiasts, who frequently call for
new space programs, new supercolliders, the Human Genome Boondoggle, the
NREN network (Al Gore's baby), and various nanotech inititatives.

As Extropians and high-tech enthusiasts, we should watch out for this
seductive argument. 

As I've said before:

"Say no to the superconducting supercollider."
"Say no to Sematech and MCC."
"Say no to the Space Station."
"Say no to the Human Genome Project."

If there are real benefits to _any_ of these efforts, they'll get pursued
by the free market, eventually, and more sustainably.

Just say no to corporate socialism.

-Tim May

