Suppose I invent a wonderful new device, say, a zipper, or better yet,
something that holds your pants shut, is never accidentally open, and
never jams. Let's call it a "clipper"
   Suppose I don't have millions of dollars in venture capital. I have
the idea and I need partners. If I scrape together a tiny little
factory, I can put my Clipper on the market, and the big guys will be
copying it within a week.
  So, I go looking for venture capitol. I contribute the idea and help
run the factory; my partners contribute money and help run the
finances, we all get rich-- or, that's my plan, anyway. I show my idea
to Vance the venture capitalist. Vance has a friend Mary who is a
mega-manufacturer. Vance turns around and *sells* my idea to Mary the
mega-manufacturer for a million dollars. I get zip.

Now, what could I have done privately? Signed a contract with Vance
that he would not expose or use my idea if I showed it to him? That's
just a private version of the contract system , and would require a
private court to enforce, and possibly a third-party neutral registry
of ideas to prove that I really *did* have the idea first and didn't
just forge a contract....
  BTW I don't see future technology as offering a way out of the
problem of forged contracts. Arguments about forged signatures may be
replaced with arguments about whether the retina-print database was
hacked.... 

