From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: PPL: Many Types of Intellectual Property Are Not Protected

Some comments on using digital time-stamping for contracts and registries
of ideas.

Elizabeth Schwartz <betsys@ra.cs.umb.edu> writes:
....
>Now, what could I have done privately? Signed a contract with Vance
>that he would not expose or use my idea if I showed it to him? That's
>just a private version of the contract system , and would require a
>private court to enforce, and possibly a third-party neutral registry
>of ideas to prove that I really *did* have the idea first and didn't
>just forge a contract....
>  BTW I don't see future technology as offering a way out of the
>problem of forged contracts. Arguments about forged signatures may be
>replaced with arguments about whether the retina-print database was
>hacked.... 

Technology _does_ offer a solution!

Digital time-stamping allows anything--ideas, songs, notebooks, contracts,
etc.--to be "registered" in a truly elegant, decentralized way. What
follows is a description.

(Caveat: Math and crypto warning! Just as Robin Hanson is well-known for
seeing almost everything in terms of idea futures and bets, I am well-known
for my crypto slant. Though less of this in the past few months, as I hate
to repeat myself on crypto basics, and the "Cypherpunks" mailing list has
been the focus of most of my crypto writings lately.)

Alice has written some text. She hashes the text with a well-known and
public hash function, such as MD-5. (Note: hash functions take a long
string and convert it into a much shorter string via various mathematical
operations. Clearly there are not as many possible strings of the shorter
length as there are strings of the longer length, so multiple long strings
can "hash to" to the same shorter string. In practice, the odds of any two
long texts hashing to, say, the same 70-digit number, are vanishingly
small. Further, many hash functions are "one-way" functions, meaning that
it is computationally infeasible to find the long string that hashed to the
short string.)

Alice could simply _publish_ this short string in a newspaper, a newspaper
sufficiently widely published and distributed (and microfilmed, and
archived on CD-ROMs, etc., as the "New York Times" is, for example) that
later attempts to publish modified versions would be "economically
infeasible." The inventors of this form of digital time-stamping, Stu Haber
and Scott Stornetta of Bellcore, call this a "widely witnessed event." 

If several years down the road someone were to challenge Alice's claim that
she wrote the text--a song, a contract, whatever--when she claims she did,
she can point to the hash number published. Unless she knows how to defeat
the one-way nature of the hash function, she could not have subsituted
another text for the one she hashed.

(Haber and Stornetta handle the issue of zillions of folks all publishing
hashed numbers in a very clever way. Alice hashes her text along with a
bunch of other numbers published in the paper. Effectively, this makes her
hashed string part of a "fabric" of such hashes, so that a would-be forger
who wants to publish phony issues of the New York Times must also replace
all of those which follow!)

Advantages:

1. The text remains private to Alice. She doesn't have to "register" it
with any authorities, private or governmental. She performs the hashing on
her own computers.

2. No trust is involved. No bribable notary publics, no corrupt officials. 

3. A fully distributed system.

Combined with other forms of crypto, these new methods will likely
revolutionize markets. It won't save state-enforced monopolies on
knowledge, though, but I've already made my points on that issue.

I won't go into some of the weaknesses, such as having folks hash _every
variant_ of an idea so that they can later point to the text of their
"right" variant and prove they can generate the published hash
value...these are very subtle arguments. Perhaps the crypto mavens will be
interested in a debate on this (probably over in Cypherpunks, though).

(For those to whom _priority_of ideas is important, they can decide to do
business with only those who can prove priority. Registries of ideas, like
registries of songs, are possible.)

-Tim May

